{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T06:51:35.462Z","ordinal":496673,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a1866d-a6a6-7d10-82ba-108fb04e2eeb","author":"/root/review_pr300_readiness","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/review_pr300_readiness\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** for PR #318.\n\nExact diff SHA-256: `56ab8a3ecef2ada575aacd09e3e5148948fb6b013640c92b071d11598a629184`.\n\n- Oracle code can bypass the approved compiler paths: the sandbox mounts all of `/usr/bin` read-only, while Python oracle code can execute arbitrary subprocesses. Setting `PATH` to prefer pinned Go/Rust roots does not prevent invoking unapproved compilers by absolute path. The new tests check PATH and mount arguments but do not exercise this escape.\n- The toolchain versions are not fixed: CI selects Go `1.22.x` and copies whichever Rust sysroot the runner provides, comparing each copy only to its source. Runner image or toolcache updates can silently change the qualification compilers.\n\nThe new mounts are read-only and checked for ownership, permissions, and symlinks; oracle caches are placed under `/tmp`, and offline settings are applied. I did not edit files or run tests.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18669-d27e-75d2-996e-5705cd6cc03e","create_time":1793256695.4626591,"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"tool","namespace":"collaboration"}}]}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":23235}}
