{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:06:57.761Z","ordinal":499905,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-48a18140-638d-4203-a2c5-32b620c93eaa","query":"ncurses terminfo binary format magic 0x011A 0x021E official documentation","action":{"type":"search","query":"ncurses terminfo binary format magic 0x011A 0x021E official documentation","queries":null},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"man.archlinux.org","ref_id":"turn9search0","snippet":"The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities that are loaded at runtime. ... By storing terminal descriptions","title":"term(5) — Arch manual pages","url":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/core/ncurses/term.5.en"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"fossies.org","ref_id":"turn9search1","snippet":"X/Open Curses does not specify a format for the terminfo database. ... The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16 bits","title":"ncurses: doc/html/man/term.5.html | Fossies","url":"https://fossies.org/linux/ncurses/doc/html/man/term.5.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"man7.org","ref_id":"turn9search2","snippet":"terminal type description in termcap format if one in terminfo ... • If the value of TERMINFO begins with “hex:” or “b64:”, ncurses ... If","title":"ncurses(3x) - Linux manual page","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/ncurses.3x.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"manpages.debian.org","ref_id":"turn9search3","snippet":"The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. ... The magic number in","title":"term(5) — ncurses-bin — Debian bookworm — Debian Manpages","url":"https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/ncurses-bin/term.5.en.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"man.archlinux.org","ref_id":"turn9search4","snippet":"If ncurses is configured with termcap support, it checks for a terminal type description in termcap format if one in terminfo format is not available.","title":"ncurses(3x) — Arch manual pages","url":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/ncurses.3x.en"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"llvm.googlesource.com","ref_id":"turn9search5","snippet":"| ** Names Section, containing the names of the terminal ... | ** values. ncurses6.1 introduced an extension to this compiled format, by ... |","title":"include/tic.h - third_party/ncurses - Git at Google","url":"https://llvm.googlesource.com/third_party/ncurses/%2B/refs/tags/v6.4/include/tic.h"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"man7.org","ref_id":"turn9search6","snippet":"This document describes ncurses version ... -mc wy30-mc Magic cookie; spaces when highlighting ... The terminfo entry consists of several capabilities, i.e., ... representative of","title":"terminfo(5) - Linux manual page","url":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/terminfo.5.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"linux.die.net","ref_id":"turn9search7","snippet":"Synonyms for the same terminal are implemented by multiple links to the same compiled file. ... If built to write hashed databases, ncurses can still","title":"term(5): format of compiled term file - Linux man page","url":"https://linux.die.net/man/5/term"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.super-man.dev","ref_id":"turn9search8","snippet":"This implementation is by default compatible with the binary terminfo format used by Solaris curses, except in a few less-used details where it was found","title":"term man page","url":"https://www.super-man.dev/man-page/file-formats-and-filesystems/term"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"man.freebsd.org","ref_id":"turn9search9","snippet":"X/Open Curses does not specify a format for the terminfo database. ... System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal de-","title":"term(5) - FreeBSD 14.0-RELEASE Manual Pages","url":"https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?apropos=0&manpath=FreeBSD+14.0-RELEASE&query=term&sektion=5"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.linux.org","ref_id":"turn9search10","snippet":"term - format of compiled term file. ... minfo/c/name where name is the name of the terminal, and c is the first character of name.","title":"term - format of compiled term file. at Linux.org","url":"https://www.linux.org/docs/man7/term.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"llvm.googlesource.com","ref_id":"turn9search11","snippet":"| terminfo format used by Solaris curses, ... | can be matched by building ncurses ... | The magic number in a binary terminfo file","title":"man/term.5 - third_party/ncurses - Git at Google","url":"https://llvm.googlesource.com/third_party/ncurses/%2B/refs/tags/v6.4/man/term.5"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"citeseerx.ist.psu.edu","ref_id":"turn9search12","snippet":"fast-loading binary description blocks under /usr/lib/terminfo. ... `terminfo' format (documented in man/terminfo.5). ... --enable-termcap option enabled, and the ncurses library cannot find a terminf","title":"This document contains licenses and notices for open source software used in","url":"https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=53caafab91ce5ebac5f67845f3687630ba3d5bca&repid=rep1&type=pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.mywebuniversity.com","ref_id":"turn9search13","snippet":"UNIX System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal description. ... This implementation is by default compatible with the binary terminfo","title":"Binary format","url":"https://www.mywebuniversity.com/Rocky_92/Man_PDF/term.5.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.uqwimax.jp","ref_id":"turn9search14","snippet":"In the 'Ada95' directory, you'll find code and documentation for an Ada95 binding of the curses API, to be used with the GNAT compiler. ...","title":"Browse the file misc/ncurses-intro.html for narrative descriptions of how to use ncurses and the panel, menu, and form libraries.","url":"https://www.uqwimax.jp/mobile/products/sar/speed_wi-fi_next_wx04/wx04_manual.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.mywebuniversity.com","ref_id":"turn9search15","snippet":"The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. ... Applications which manipulate terminal","title":"The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. This extension is made possible by using the fact that the other implementations stop reading the terminfo data when they have reached the end of the size given in the header. ncurses checks the size, and if it exceeds that due to the predefined data, continues to parse according to its own scheme.","url":"https://www.mywebuniversity.com/Man_PDF/term.5.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"device.report","ref_id":"turn9search16","snippet":"`terminfo' format (documented in man/terminfo.5). ... either /etc/termcap or the compiled binary terminfo blocks, but prefers the ... --enable-termcap option enabled, and the ncurses library","title":"2CDC502082D0101_PH_IPRS_311_DE_V1-0","url":"https://device.report/m/58b4bf5d0ad310e9ee0e31ffbdc6622aa4fe381376a9baa8e66ee9f4710d39a7.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.mywebuniversity.com","ref_id":"turn9search17","snippet":"terminfo(5) File Formats terminfo(5) ... terminfo - terminal capability data base ... tions, using high-level calls to libraries such as curses(3X). ... This manual describes","title":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux Release 9.2 Manual Pages on 'terminfo.5' command","url":"https://www.mywebuniversity.com/RedHat_92/Man_PDF/terminfo.5.pdf"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"en.wikipedia.org","ref_id":"turn9search18","snippet":"Terminfo data is stored as a binary file, making it less simple to modify than termcap. ... X/Open does not specify the format of the","title":"Terminfo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminfo"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"en.wikipedia.org","ref_id":"turn9search19","snippet":"Developers | Thomas E. ... However, a few areas are problematic, such as handling terminal resizing, since no counterpart exists in the old curses. ...","title":"Ncurses","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ncurses"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"www.reddit.com","ref_id":"turn9reddit20","snippet":"In Connection -> Data -> Terminal-type string you can set that for 'xterm-256color' if it's not already set. ... If the problem is in nethack","title":"If you're having trouble using numpad in curses mode...","url":"https://www.reddit.com/r/nethack/comments/15bk890"}]},"started_at_ms":1793275616706,"completed_at_ms":1793275617761}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:06:57.878Z","ordinal":499906,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1878e-6256-7592-8aba-f43dd0b24fb1","call_id":"call_fae6918bbfab434f87098013cae1bafd","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"term(5) — Arch manual pages (https://man.archlinux.org/man/core/ncurses/term.5.en)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities that are loaded at runtime. ... By storing terminal descriptions in this way, ncurses is able to provide a database useful with legacy applications, as well as providing data for applications that require more information about a terminal type than was anticipated by X/Open Curses. ... The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16 bits (two bytes).\n# FILES\n\n/usr/share/terminfo\n    compiled terminal description database\n\n# PORTABILITY\n\n## setupterm\n\nNote that it is possible for setupterm to expect a different set of capabilities than are actually present in the file. Either the database may have been updated since setupterm was recompiled (resulting in extra unrecognized entries in the file) or the program may have been recompiled more recently than the database was updated (resulting in missing entries). The routine setupterm must be prepared for both possibilities - this is why the numbers and sizes are included. Also, new capabilities must always be added at the end of the lists of Boolean, number, and string capabilities.\n\n## Binary Format\n\nX/Open Curses does not specify a format for the terminfo database. System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal description.\n\nDespite the consistent use of little-endian numbers and the otherwise self-describing format, it is not wise to count on portability of binary terminfo entries between commercial Unix versions. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nncurses: doc/html/man/term.5.html | Fossies (https://fossies.org/linux/ncurses/doc/html/man/term.5.html)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 10 months ago;            X/Open Curses does not specify a  format  for  the  terminfo  database. ...            The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first  16  bits  (two ...            ncurses stores compiled terminal descriptions in three related formats,\n## FILES\n    \n           /usr/share/terminfo\n                  compiled terminal description database\n\n## PORTABILITY\n    \n    \n    \n\n### setupterm\n    \n           Note  that  it  is  possible for setupterm to expect a different set of\n           capabilities than  are  actually  present  in  the  file.   Either  the\n           database   may   have  been  updated  since  setupterm  was  recompiled\n           (resulting in extra unrecognized entries in the file)  or  the  program\n           may  have  been  recompiled more recently than the database was updated\n           (resulting in missing entries).  The routine setupterm must be prepared\n           for both possibilities  -  this  is  why  the  numbers  and  sizes  are\n           included.   Also,  new  capabilities must always be added at the end of\n           the lists of Boolean, number, and string capabilities.\n    \n    \n    \n\n### Binary Format\n    \n           X/Open Curses does not specify a  format  for  the  terminfo  database.\n           System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nncurses(3x) - Linux manual page (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/ncurses.3x.html)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;            terminal type description in termcap format if one in terminfo ...            •   If the value of TERMINFO begins with “hex:” or “b64:”, ncurses ...            If TERMCAP does not hold a terminal type description or file name,\n               rather than /usr/share/terminfo/.\n    \n           The hashed database uses less disk space and is a little faster\n           than the directory tree.  However, some applications assume the\n           existence of the directory tree and read it directly, ignoring the\n           terminfo API.\n    \n           •   If ncurses is configured with termcap support, this variable\n               may contain the location of a termcap file.\n    \n           •   If the value of TERMINFO begins with “hex:” or “b64:”, ncurses\n               uses the remainder of the value as a compiled terminfo\n               description.  You might produce the base64 format using\n               infocmp(1M).\n    \n                      TERMINFO=$(infocmp -0 -Q2 -q)\n                      export TERMINFO\n    \n               ncurses uses the compiled description only if it corresponds\n               to the terminal type identified by TERM.\n    \n           Setting TERMINFO is the simplest, but not the only, way to direct\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm(5) — ncurses-bin — Debian bookworm — Debian Manpages (https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/ncurses-bin/term.5.en.html)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.3 years ago; The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. ... The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16-bits (two bytes). ... If the underlying filesystem ignores the difference between uppercase and lowercase, ncurses represents the “first character” of the terminal name used as the intermediate level of a directory tree in (two-character) hexadecimal form.\n# PORTABILITY¶\n\n## setupterm¶\n\nNote that it is possible for setupterm to expect a different set of capabilities than are actually present in the file. Either the database may have been updated since setupterm was recompiled (resulting in extra unrecognized entries in the file) or the program may have been recompiled more recently than the database was updated (resulting in missing entries). The routine setupterm must be prepared for both possibilities - this is why the numbers and sizes are included. Also, new capabilities must always be added at the end of the lists of boolean, number, and string capabilities.\n\n## Binary format¶\n\nX/Open Curses does not specify a format for the terminfo database. UNIX System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal description.\n\nDespite the consistent use of little-endian for numbers and the otherwise self-describing format, it is not wise to count on portability of binary terminfo entries between commercial UNIX versions. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nncurses(3x) — Arch manual pages (https://man.archlinux.org/man/ncurses.3x.en)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: yesterday; If ncurses is configured with termcap support, it checks for a terminal type description in termcap format if one in terminfo format is not available. ...   * If ncurses is configured with termcap support, this variable may contain the location of a termcap file.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ninclude/tic.h - third_party/ncurses - Git at Google (https://llvm.googlesource.com/third_party/ncurses/%2B/refs/tags/v6.4/include/tic.h)\nciteturn9search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;  | ** Names Section, containing the names of the terminal ...  | ** values. ncurses6.1 introduced an extension to this compiled format, by ...  | ** NOTE that all short integers in the file are stored using VAX/PDP-style ... Terminfo format is a raw byte layout, not a structure ...  | #define MAGIC 0432 /* first two bytes of a compiled entry */\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterminfo(5) - Linux manual page (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/terminfo.5.html)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;            This document describes ncurses version ...            -mc      wy30-mc     Magic cookie; spaces when highlighting ...            The terminfo entry consists of several capabilities, i.e., ...            representative of what a terminfo entry for a modern terminal ...                    acsc=+\\020\\,\\021-\\030. ...                Modifying this would require a new binary format, which would\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm(5): format of compiled term file - Linux man page (https://linux.die.net/man/5/term)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Synonyms for the same terminal are implemented by multiple links to the same compiled file. ...     If built to write hashed databases, ncurses can still read terminfo databases organized as a directory tree, but cannot write entries into the directory tree. ...     (1) the magic number (octal 0432); ... The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. ... After the end of these values, it contains the names for each of the extended capabilities in order, e.g., booleans, then numbers and finally strings.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm man page (https://www.super-man.dev/man-page/file-formats-and-filesystems/term)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; This implementation is by default compatible with the binary terminfo format used by Solaris curses, except in a few less-used details where it was found that the latter did not match X/Open Curses. ... The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16-bits (two bytes). ... If the underlying filesystem ignores the difference between uppercase and lowercase, ncurses represents the “first character” of the terminal name used as the intermediate level of a directory tree in (two-character) hexadecimal form.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm(5) - FreeBSD 14.0-RELEASE Manual Pages (https://man.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?apropos=0&manpath=FreeBSD+14.0-RELEASE&query=term&sektion=5)\nciteturn9search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago;          X/Open Curses does not specify a format for the  terminfo\tdatabase. ...          System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal de- ...          The  magic  number  in  a\tbinary\tterminfo  file is the first 16-bits (two\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm - format of compiled term file. at Linux.org (https://www.linux.org/docs/man7/term.html)\nciteturn9search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9.4 years ago;            term - format of compiled term file. ...                 minfo/c/name  where  name  is the name of the terminal, and c is the first character of name. ...                 If  built  to  write hashed databases, ncurses can still read terminfo databases organized as a directory ...                 (1) the magic number (octal 0432);\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nman/term.5 - third_party/ncurses - Git at Google (https://llvm.googlesource.com/third_party/ncurses/%2B/refs/tags/v6.4/man/term.5)\nciteturn9search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;  | terminfo format used by Solaris curses, ...  | can be matched by building ncurses ...  | The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16-bits (two bytes). ...  | described in \\fBcurs_terminfo\\fP(3X) for reading terminal capabilities.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis document contains licenses and notices for open source software used in (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=53caafab91ce5ebac5f67845f3687630ba3d5bca&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn9search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; fast-loading binary description blocks under /usr/lib/terminfo. ... `terminfo' format (documented in man/terminfo.5). ... --enable-termcap option enabled, and the ncurses library cannot find a terminfo \ndescription for your terminal, it will fall back to the termcap file supplied ... Detailed documentation for all libraries and utilities can be found in the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBinary format (https://www.mywebuniversity.com/Rocky_92/Man_PDF/term.5.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; UNIX System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal description. ... This implementation is by default compatible with the binary terminfo format used by Solaris curses, except in a few less-used details where it was found that the latter did not match X/Open Curses. ... The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first 16-bits (two bytes).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBrowse the file misc/ncurses-intro.html for narrative descriptions of how to use ncurses and the panel, menu, and form libraries. (https://www.uqwimax.jp/mobile/products/sar/speed_wi-fi_next_wx04/wx04_manual.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Crawled: 8.5 years ago; In the 'Ada95' directory, you'll find code and documentation for an Ada95 binding of the curses API, to be used with the GNAT compiler. ... These binary blocks are compiled from an improved editable text representation called 'terminfo' format (documented in man/terminfo.5). ... If the package was built with the --enable-termcap option enabled, and the ncurses library cannot find a terminfo description for your terminal, it will fall back to the termcap file supplied with your system (which the ncurses package installation leaves strictly alone).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. This extension is made possible by using the fact that the other implementations stop reading the terminfo data when they have reached the end of the size given in the header. ncurses checks the size, and if it exceeds that due to the predefined data, continues to parse according to its own scheme. (https://www.mywebuniversity.com/Man_PDF/term.5.pdf)\nciteturn9search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; The ncurses libraries and applications support extended terminfo binary format, allowing users to define capabilities which are loaded at runtime. ... Applications which manipulate terminal data can use the definitions described in term_variables(3X) which associate the long capability names with members of a TERMTYPE structure. ... a different magic number (octal 01036)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2CDC502082D0101_PH_IPRS_311_DE_V1-0 (https://device.report/m/58b4bf5d0ad310e9ee0e31ffbdc6622aa4fe381376a9baa8e66ee9f4710d39a7.pdf)\nciteturn9search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; `terminfo' format (documented in man/terminfo.5). ... either /etc/termcap or the compiled binary terminfo blocks, but prefers the ... --enable-termcap option enabled, and the ncurses library cannot find a terminfo \ndescription for your terminal, it will fall back to the termcap file supplied\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRed Hat Enterprise Linux Release 9.2 Manual Pages on 'terminfo.5' command (https://www.mywebuniversity.com/RedHat_92/Man_PDF/terminfo.5.pdf)\nciteturn9search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; terminfo(5)                      File Formats                      terminfo(5) ...        terminfo - terminal capability data base ...        tions,  using  high-level calls to libraries such as curses(3X). ...        This manual describes ncurses version 6.2 (patch 20210508).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTerminfo (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminfo)\nciteturn9search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Terminfo data is stored as a binary file, making it less simple to modify than termcap. ... X/Open does not specify the format of the compiled terminal description. ... For example, ncurses can be built to match the terminfo data for several other implementations. ... However, most implementations note in their documentation for tic (terminal information compiler) that compiled entries cannot exceed 4,096 bytes in size.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNcurses (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ncurses)\nciteturn9search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Developers | Thomas E. ... However, a few areas are problematic, such as handling terminal resizing, since no counterpart exists in the old curses. ... ncurses can use either terminfo (with extensible data) or termcap. ... * conio.h – A C header file used mostly with MS-DOS compilers to create text user interfaces ... * Official website ... *  ooRexx Documentation 5.0.0: nCurses Class Library Reference (PDF).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIf you're having trouble using numpad in curses mode... (https://www.reddit.com/r/nethack/comments/15bk890)\nciteturn9reddit20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: 2.0 years ago; In Connection -> Data -> Terminal-type string you can set that for 'xterm-256color' if it's not already set. ... If the problem is in nethack rather than in ncurses, it might be that the termcap/terminfo entry is telling the terminal to send 8-bit escape sequences.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","create_time":1793275617.8784065}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
