{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:07:04.516Z","ordinal":499918,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae336e600b8819fb048d0e59ba6b7e7","usage":{"input_tokens":236470,"cached_input_tokens":232192,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":206,"reasoning_output_tokens":130,"total_tokens":236676},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":27437642,"cached_input_tokens":26962688,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":58594,"reasoning_output_tokens":33245,"total_tokens":27496236},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":9351555442,"cached_input_tokens":9251660416,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":27090900,"reasoning_output_tokens":15896458,"total_tokens":9378646342}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:07:05.080Z","ordinal":499919,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-6c3e9851-ae13-4543-9dfb-815c69197a46","query":"'different magic number'","action":{"type":"findInPage","url":null,"pattern":"different magic number"},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"invisible-island.net","ref_id":"turn11view0","snippet":"Total lines: 362","title":"term 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats","url":"https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"invisible-island.net","ref_id":"turn11view1","snippet":"Total lines: 362","title":"term 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats","url":"https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"invisible-island.net","ref_id":"turn11view2","snippet":"Total lines: 362","title":"term 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats","url":"https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html"}]},"started_at_ms":1793275624497,"completed_at_ms":1793275625080}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:07:05.211Z","ordinal":499920,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1878e-7efa-7881-ad42-213c406eaddc","call_id":"call_78bb131e2e6f4ebcb096cf94b13e983a","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.7 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"term 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats (https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn10search0\",\"pattern\":\"different magic number\"}); Total lines: 362\nL53: ### Legacy Storage Format\nL54: \nL55:            The format has been chosen so that it will be the same on all hardware.\nL56:            A  byte  of  at  least eight bits' width is assumed, but no assumptions\nL57:            about bit ordering or sign extension are made.\nL58: \nL59:            The file is divided into six parts:\nL60: \nL61:                 (a) header,\nL62: \nL63:                 (b) terminal names,\nL64: \nL65:                 (c) Boolean flags,\nL66: \nL67:                 (d) numbers,\nL68: \nL69:                 (e) strings, and\nL70:                 (f) a string table.\nL71: \nL72:            The header section begins the file.  This section  contains  six  short\nL73:            integers in the format described below.  These integers are\nL74: \nL75:                 (1) the magic number\nL76:                      (octal 0432);\nL77: \nL78:                 (2) the size,\nL79:                      in bytes, of the terminal names section;\nL80: \nL81:                 (3) the number of bytes in the Boolean flags section;\nL82:                 (4) the number of short integers in the numbers section;\nL83: \nL84:                 (5) the number of offsets\nL85:                      (short integers) in the strings section;\nL86: \nL87:                 (6) the size,\nL88:                      in bytes, of the string table.\nL89: \nL90:            The  capabilities  in  the Boolean flags, numbers, and strings sections\nL91:            are in the same order as in the header file term.h.\nL92: \nL93:            Short integers are signed, in the range -32768 to 32767, and stored  in\nL94:            little-endian format.\nL95: \nL96:            Numbers  in  a  terminal  description,  whether they are entries in the\nL97:            numbers or strings table, are positive  integers.   Boolean  flags  are\nL98:            treated  as  positive  one-byte integers.  In each case, those positive\nL99:            integers represent a terminal capability.  The  terminal  compiler  tic\nL100:            uses  negative  integers  to handle the cases where a capability is not\nL101:            available:\nL171:            extended capability names as well as extended capability values.\nL172: \nL173:            Using the counts and sizes, ncurses allocates arrays and reads data for\nL174:            the extended capabilities in the same order as the header information.\nL175: \nL176:            The  extended  string  table  contains  values for string capabilities.\nL177:            After the end of these values, it contains the names for  each  of  the\nL178:            extended capabilities in order: Boolean, numeric, and string.\nL179:            By  storing  terminal  descriptions  in  this  way,  ncurses is able to\nL180:            provide  a  database  useful  with  legacy  applications,  as  well  as\nL181:            providing  data  for applications that require more information about a\nL182:            terminal type than was anticipated by X/Open Curses.  See  【6†user_caps(5)】\nL183:            for an overview of the way ncurses uses this extended information.\nL184: \nL185:            Applications  that  manipulate  terminal  data  can use the definitions\nL186:            described in 【7†term_variables(3x)】 associating the long  capability  names\nL187:            with members of a TERMTYPE structure.\nL188: ### Extended Number Format\nL189: \nL190:            On  occasion, 16-bit signed integers are not large enough.  ncurses 6.1\nL191:            introduced a new format by making a few changes to the legacy format:\nL192: \nL193:            o   a different magic number (octal 01036)\nL194: \nL195:            o   changing the type for the number array from signed 16-bit  integers\nL196:                to signed 32-bit integers.\nL197: \nL198:            To   maintain   compatibility,  the  library  presents  the  same  data\nL199:            structures to direct users of the TERMTYPE  structure  as  in  previous\nL200:            formats.   However,  that  cannot  provide  callers  with  the extended\nL201:            numbers.   The  library  uses  a  similar  but  hidden  data  structure\nL202:            TERMTYPE2 to provide data for the terminfo functions.\nL203: ## FILES\nL204: \nL205:            /usr/share/terminfo\nL206:                   compiled terminal description database\nL207: \nL208: \nL209: \nL210: \nL211: ## PORTABILITY\nL212: ### setupterm\nL213: \nL214:            Note  that  it  is  possible for setupterm to expect a different set of\nL215:            capabilities than  are  actually  present  in  the  file.   Either  the\nL216:            database   may   have  been  updated  since  setupterm  was  recompiled\nL217:            (resulting in extra unrecognized entries in the file)  or  the  program\nL218:            may  have  been  recompiled more recently than the database was updated\nL219:            (resulting in missing entries).  The routine setupterm must be prepared\nL220:            for both possibilities  -  this  is  why  the  numbers  and  sizes  are\nL221:            included.   Also,  new  capabilities must always be added at the end of\nL222:            the lists of Boolean, number, and string capabilities.\nL223: ### Binary Format\nL224: \nL225:            X/Open Curses does not specify a  format  for  the  terminfo  database.\nL226:            System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal\nL227:            description.\nL228: \nL229:            Despite  the  consistent use of little-endian numbers and the otherwise\nL230:            self-describing format, it is not  wise  to  count  on  portability  of\nL231:            binary  terminfo entries between commercial Unix versions.  The problem\nL232:            is that there are at least three versions  of  terminfo  (under  HP-UX,\nL233:            AIX,  and  OSF/1)  each  of which diverged from System V terminfo after\nL234:            SVr1, and added extension capabilities to the string table that (in the\nL235:            binary format) collide with System V and X/Open Curses extensions.  See\nL236:            【8†terminfo(5)】 for detailed discussion of  terminfo  source  compatibility\nL237:            issues.\nL238:            This  implementation  is by default compatible with the binary terminfo\nL239:            format used by Solaris curses, except in a few less-used details  where\nL240:            it  was  found that the latter did not match X/Open Curses.  The format\nL241:            used by the other Unix versions can be matched by building ncurses with\nL242:            different configuration options.\nL243: ### Magic Codes\nL244: \nL245:            The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first  16  bits  (two\nL246:            bytes).   Besides making it more reliable for the library to check that\nL247:            a file is terminfo, utilities such as 【9†file(1)†pubs.opengroup.org】 also  use  that  to  tell\nL248:            what  the file-format is.  System V defined more than one magic number,\nL249:            with 0433, 0435 as screen-dumps (see 【10†scr_dump(5)】).  This implementation\nL250:            uses 01036 as a continuation of that sequence,  but  with  a  different\nL251:            high-order byte to avoid confusion.\nL252: ### The TERMTYPE Structure\nL253: \nL254:            Direct  access  to  the  TERMTYPE  structure  is  provided  for  legacy\nL255:            applications.   Portable  applications  should  use  【5†tigetflag(3x)】  and\nL256:            related functions to read terminal capabilities.\nL257: ### Mixed-case Terminal Names\nL258: \nL259:            A  small  number  of  terminal descriptions use uppercase characters in\nL260:            their names.  If the underlying  file  system  ignores  the  difference\nL261:            between   uppercase   and  lowercase,  ncurses  represents  the  \"first\nL262:            character\" of the terminal name used as the  intermediate  level  of  a\nL263:            directory tree in (two-character) hexadecimal form.\nL264: ### Limits\nL265: \nL266:            ncurses stores compiled terminal descriptions in three related formats,\nL267:            described in the subsections\nL268: \nL269:            o   Legacy Storage Format, and\nL270: \nL271:            o   Extended Storage Format, and\nL272: \nL273:            o   Extended Number Format.\nL274: \nL275:            The  legacy storage format and the extended number format differ by the\nL276:            types of numeric capability that  they  can  store  (for  example,  16-\nL277:            versus  32-bit  integers).   The  extended storage format introduced by\nL278:            ncurses 5.0 adds data to either of these formats.\nL279: \nL280:            Some limitations apply:\nL281: \nL282:            o   total compiled entries cannot  exceed  4096  bytes  in  the  legacy\nL283:                format.\nL284: \nL285:            o   total  compiled  entries  cannot exceed 32768 bytes in the extended\nL286:                format.\nL287: \nL288:            o   the name field cannot exceed 128 bytes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats (https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn10search0\",\"pattern\":\"0x021E\"}); Total lines: 362\nNo matching text found for \"0x021E\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nterm 5 2025-08-16 ncurses 6.6 File formats (https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/man/term.5.html)\nciteturn11view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn10search0\",\"pattern\":\"01036\"}); Total lines: 362\nL188: ### Extended Number Format\nL189: \nL190:            On  occasion, 16-bit signed integers are not large enough.  ncurses 6.1\nL191:            introduced a new format by making a few changes to the legacy format:\nL192: \nL193:            o   a different magic number (octal 01036)\nL194: \nL195:            o   changing the type for the number array from signed 16-bit  integers\nL196:                to signed 32-bit integers.\nL197: \nL198:            To   maintain   compatibility,  the  library  presents  the  same  data\nL212: ### setupterm\nL213: \nL219:            (resulting in missing entries).  The routine setupterm must be prepared\nL220:            for both possibilities  -  this  is  why  the  numbers  and  sizes  are\nL221:            included.   Also,  new  capabilities must always be added at the end of\nL222:            the lists of Boolean, number, and string capabilities.\nL223: ### Binary Format\nL224: \nL225:            X/Open Curses does not specify a  format  for  the  terminfo  database.\nL226:            System V curses used a directory-tree of binary files, one per terminal\nL227:            description.\nL228: \nL229:            Despite  the  consistent use of little-endian numbers and the otherwise\nL230:            self-describing format, it is not  wise  to  count  on  portability  of\nL231:            binary  terminfo entries between commercial Unix versions.  The problem\nL232:            is that there are at least three versions  of  terminfo  (under  HP-UX,\nL233:            AIX,  and  OSF/1)  each  of which diverged from System V terminfo after\nL234:            SVr1, and added extension capabilities to the string table that (in the\nL235:            binary format) collide with System V and X/Open Curses extensions.  See\nL236:            【8†terminfo(5)】 for detailed discussion of  terminfo  source  compatibility\nL237:            issues.\nL238:            This  implementation  is by default compatible with the binary terminfo\nL239:            format used by Solaris curses, except in a few less-used details  where\nL240:            it  was  found that the latter did not match X/Open Curses.  The format\nL241:            used by the other Unix versions can be matched by building ncurses with\nL242:            different configuration options.\nL243: ### Magic Codes\nL244: \nL245:            The magic number in a binary terminfo file is the first  16  bits  (two\nL246:            bytes).   Besides making it more reliable for the library to check that\nL247:            a file is terminfo, utilities such as 【9†file(1)†pubs.opengroup.org】 also  use  that  to  tell\nL248:            what  the file-format is.  System V defined more than one magic number,\nL249:            with 0433, 0435 as screen-dumps (see 【10†scr_dump(5)】).  This implementation\nL250:            uses 01036 as a continuation of that sequence,  but  with  a  different\nL251:            high-order byte to avoid confusion.\nL252: ### The TERMTYPE Structure\nL253: \nL254:            Direct  access  to  the  TERMTYPE  structure  is  provided  for  legacy\nL255:            applications.   Portable  applications  should  use  【5†tigetflag(3x)】  and\nL256:            related functions to read terminal capabilities.\nL257: ### Mixed-case Terminal Names\nL258: \nL259:            A  small  number  of  terminal descriptions use uppercase characters in\nL260:            their names.  If the underlying  file  system  ignores  the  difference\nL261:            between   uppercase   and  lowercase,  ncurses  represents  the  \"first\nL262:            character\" of the terminal name used as the  intermediate  level  of  a\nL263:            directory tree in (two-character) hexadecimal form.\nL264: ### Limits\nL265: \nL266:            ncurses stores compiled terminal descriptions in three related formats,\nL267:            described in the subsections\nL268: \nL269:            o   Legacy Storage Format, and\nL270: \nL271:            o   Extended Storage Format, and\nL272: \nL273:            o   Extended Number Format.\nL274: \nL275:            The  legacy storage format and the extended number format differ by the\nL276:            types of numeric capability that  they  can  store  (for  example,  16-\nL277:            versus  32-bit  integers).   The  extended storage format introduced by\nL278:            ncurses 5.0 adds data to either of these formats.\nL279: \nL280:            Some limitations apply:\nL281: \nL282:            o   total compiled entries cannot  exceed  4096  bytes  in  the  legacy\nL283:                format.\nL284: \nL285:            o   total  compiled  entries  cannot exceed 32768 bytes in the extended\nL286:                format.\nL287: \nL288:            o   the name field cannot exceed 128 bytes.\nL289:            Compiled entries are limited to 32768 bytes because  offsets  into  the\nL290:            strings  table  use  two-byte  integers.   The legacy format could have\nL291:            supported 32768-byte entries, but  was  limited  to  a  virtual  memory\nL292:            page's 4096 bytes.\nL293: ## EXAMPLES\nL294: \nL295:            Here  is  a  terminfo  description of the Lear-Siegler ADM-3, a popular\nL296:            though rather stupid early terminal.\nL297: \nL298:            adm3a|lsi adm3a,\nL299:                    am,\nL300:                    cols#80, lines#24,\nL301:                    bel=^G, clear=\\032$<1>, cr=^M, cub1=^H, cud1=^J,\nL302:                    cuf1=^L, cup=\\E=%p1%{32}%+%c%p2%{32}%+%c, cuu1=^K,\nL303:                    home=^^, ind=^J,\nL304: \nL305:            A hexadecimal dump of its  compiled  terminal  description  (in  legacy\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","create_time":1793275625.2109873}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
