{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T12:43:56.650Z","ordinal":501419,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0db389702a7164cc016ae33f8beab8819f866961336d7344a6","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"The fresh review found one more concrete escape: a system compiler symlink can point outside scanned system roots, then become reachable if the candidate separately mounts that target as an approved runtime path. I’m checking the actual mount construction and call sites now; the focused tests that passed did not cover this path, so PR #318 remains blocked."}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1793277835942,"completed_at_ms":1793277836650}}
