{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T13:16:37.447Z","ordinal":502919,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae3472cedc4819fafb1010eeabefcb7","usage":{"input_tokens":180540,"cached_input_tokens":179840,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":908,"reasoning_output_tokens":757,"total_tokens":181448},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":89136296,"cached_input_tokens":88040192,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":228584,"reasoning_output_tokens":145827,"total_tokens":89364880},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":9413254096,"cached_input_tokens":9312737920,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":27260890,"reasoning_output_tokens":16009040,"total_tokens":9440514986}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T13:16:38.438Z","ordinal":502920,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-af9c3dca-f3dc-4962-addc-c2a6bbf3d3a8","process_id":"9489","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","nl -ba eval/validate.mjs | sed -n '1,90p'; nl -ba eval/validate.mjs | sed -n '160,225p'; nl -ba eval/oracle.mjs | sed -n '1,75p'; rg -n -i 'oracle.*runtime|runtime.*oracle|hidden.*oracle|black.?box.*check|toolchain' docs/v1.0.0 | head -100"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/eval-agent-toolchain-binds","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba eval/validate.mjs","name":"validate.mjs","path":"eval/validate.mjs"},{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba eval/oracle.mjs","name":"oracle.mjs","path":"eval/oracle.mjs"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n -i 'oracle.*runtime|runtime.*oracle|hidden.*oracle|black.?box.*check|toolchain' docs/v1.0.0","query":"oracle.*runtime|runtime.*oracle|hidden.*oracle|black.?box.*check|toolchain","path":"v1.0.0"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"     1\t// Checks that make an evaluation run valid before it starts (SPEC-10, REQ-QUAL-101/102/106): every task is well formed,\n     2\t// a qualification run records which model actually answered (a fingerprint, not an alias), and hidden tests are out of\n     3\t// the agent's reach when the agent runs as its own user.\n     4\timport fs from \"node:fs\";\n     5\timport path from \"node:path\";\n     6\timport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n     7\timport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\n     8\timport { assertNoSymlinks, candidateSandboxHostMountSources, confinedPath, runtimeMountProblem, sandboxAvailable } from \"./isolate.mjs\";\n     9\timport { blackBoxOracleProblem } from \"./oracle.mjs\";\n    10\timport { t3TaskProblems } from \"./t3.mjs\";\n    11\timport { resourceProbeProblem } from \"./resources.mjs\";\n    12\timport { t4FixtureProblems } from \"./t4_fixtures.mjs\";\n    13\t\n    14\texport const TIERS = [\"T1\", \"T2\", \"T3\", \"T4\"];\n    15\texport const CLASSES = [\"bugfix\", \"feature\", \"refactor\", \"debug\"];\n    16\texport const DIFFICULTIES = [\"easy\", \"medium\", \"hard\"];\n    17\tconst ROOT = path.resolve(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), \"..\");\n    18\t\n    19\t/** Problems with one task.json (empty when valid). `qualification` adds the fields held-out tasks must carry. */\n    20\texport function validateTask(task, dir, { qualification = false } = {}) {\n    21\t  const problems = [];\n    22\t  const need = (cond, msg) => { if (!cond) problems.push(msg); };\n    23\t  need(typeof task.id === \"string\" && task.id === path.basename(dir), `id must equal its directory name (${path.basename(dir)})`);\n    24\t  need(TIERS.includes(task.tier), `tier must be one of ${TIERS.join(\", \")}`);\n    25\t  need(typeof task.objective === \"string\" && task.objective.trim().length > 0, \"objective is required\");\n    26\t  problems.push(...t3TaskProblems(task));\n    27\t  need(Boolean(task.fixture) !== Boolean(task.repo), \"exactly one of fixture or repo is required\");\n    28\t  if (task.fixture) {\n    29\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.fixture); }\n    30\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    31\t  }\n    32\t  if (task.repo) {\n    33\t    need(typeof task.repo.url === \"string\" && task.repo.url.length > 0, \"repo.url is required\");\n    34\t    try {\n    35\t      const repoUrl = new URL(task.repo.url);\n    36\t      need(repoUrl.protocol === \"https:\" && !repoUrl.username && !repoUrl.password, \"repo.url must use HTTPS without embedded credentials\");\n    37\t    } catch { need(false, \"repo.url must be a valid HTTPS URL without embedded credentials\"); }\n    38\t    need(/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/.test(task.repo.commit ?? \"\"), \"repo.commit must be a full 40-character commit id (pinned)\");\n    39\t  }\n    40\t  const hiddenCommand = typeof task.hidden?.command === \"string\" && task.hidden.command.length > 0;\n    41\t  const hiddenOracle = task.hidden?.protocol === \"blackbox-v1\";\n    42\t  need(hiddenCommand || hiddenOracle, \"hidden.command or hidden blackbox-v1 oracle is required\");\n    43\t  if (hiddenOracle) {\n    44\t    const oracleProblem = blackBoxOracleProblem(task.hidden, dir, task.fixture ?? null);\n    45\t    need(!oracleProblem, oracleProblem ?? \"hidden black-box oracle is invalid\");\n    46\t  }\n    47\t  // Legacy validators execute candidate modules in the same runtime. File hiding and a fresh workspace cannot prevent\n    48\t  // candidate code from inspecting or mutating that runtime, so they are never valid independent qualification.\n    49\t  if (qualification) need(hiddenOracle && !hiddenCommand, \"qualification requires the isolated blackbox-v1 hidden oracle; in-process legacy validators are not accepted\");\n    50\t  if (task.hidden?.dir) {\n    51\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.hidden.dir); }\n    52\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    53\t  }\n    54\t  if (task.regression?.dir) {\n    55\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.regression.dir); }\n    56\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    57\t  }\n    58\t  for (const key of [\"steps\", \"minutes\"]) {\n    59\t    if (task[key] !== undefined) need(Number.isInteger(task[key]) && task[key] > 0, `${key} must be a positive integer`);\n    60\t  }\n    61\t  if (task.faults !== undefined) {\n    62\t    const kinds = [\"refuse\", \"overloaded\", \"stream_cut\", \"truncate\", \"context_overflow\", \"malformed_tool_json\"];\n    63\t    need(Array.isArray(task.faults) && task.faults.every((f) => kinds.includes(f?.kind) && Number.isInteger(f?.atRequest) && f.atRequest >= 1), `faults must be [{ kind: ${kinds.join(\"|\")}, atRequest: n >= 1 }]`);\n    64\t  }\n    65\t  if (task.t4RunnerFault !== undefined) {\n    66\t    need(task.tier === \"T4\" && task.t4RunnerFault === \"sigterm_resume\", \"t4RunnerFault is supported only as sigterm_resume on a T4 task\");\n    67\t  }\n    68\t  problems.push(...t4FixtureProblems(task));\n    69\t  if (task.class !== undefined || qualification) need(CLASSES.includes(task.class), `class must be one of ${CLASSES.join(\", \")}`);\n    70\t  if (task.difficulty !== undefined || qualification) need(DIFFICULTIES.includes(task.difficulty), `difficulty must be one of ${DIFFICULTIES.join(\", \")}`);\n    71\t  return problems;\n    72\t}\n    73\t\n    74\t/**\n    75\t * What a run's model is, beyond its alias: from the endpoint's /models entry (id, owner, advertised context) and the\n    76\t * operator's config (`fingerprint`: weights sha256, server build; `sampling`: temperature, seed). A qualification config\n    77\t * that cannot state weights, server build and sampling is refused (REQ-QUAL-102).\n    78\t */\n    79\texport function modelFingerprint(config, entry) {\n    80\t  const fp = config.fingerprint ?? {};\n    81\t  const sampling = config.sampling ?? null;\n    82\t  const fingerprint = {\n    83\t    alias: config.model,\n    84\t    listed: Boolean(entry),\n    85\t    ownedBy: entry?.owned_by ?? null,\n    86\t    contextLength: entry?.context_length ?? entry?.max_model_len ?? entry?.meta?.n_ctx_train ?? null,\n    87\t    weightsSha256: fp.weightsSha256 ?? null,\n    88\t    serverBuild: fp.serverBuild ?? null,\n    89\t    sampling,\n    90\t  };\n   160\t    } catch {\n   161\t      const parent = path.dirname(current);\n   162\t      if (parent === current) return { lexical, canonical: lexical };\n   163\t      suffix.unshift(path.basename(current));\n   164\t      current = parent;\n   165\t    }\n   166\t  }\n   167\t}\n   168\t\n   169\tfunction overlapVariants(left, right) {\n   170\t  return [left.lexical, left.canonical].some((a) => [right.lexical, right.canonical].some((b) => pathOverlaps(a, b)));\n   171\t}\n   172\t\n   173\t/** Refuse candidate runtime mounts that could expose the task tree or its private corpus store. */\n   174\texport function runtimeCorpusOverlapProblem(runtimePaths, runtimeRoots, tasksDir, extraPrivatePaths = [], mountContext = {}) {\n   175\t  if (!Array.isArray(runtimePaths) || !Array.isArray(runtimeRoots) || !Array.isArray(extraPrivatePaths)) {\n   176\t    return \"qualification runtime and corpus paths must be explicit arrays\";\n   177\t  }\n   178\t  const taskPath = resolveCanonicalOrLexical(tasksDir);\n   179\t  if (!taskPath || !fs.existsSync(tasksDir)) return \"qualification task corpus path is unavailable\";\n   180\t  // Held-out stores keep tasks/, references/, and custody material under one directory. Keep that parent private\n   181\t  // outside the in-repo development eval/tasks tree, where eval/ also contains the trusted sandbox supervisor.\n   182\t  const parentDir = resolveCanonicalOrLexical(path.dirname(tasksDir));\n   183\t  const evalDir = resolveCanonicalOrLexical(path.join(ROOT, \"eval\"));\n   184\t  const includeParent = mountContext.qualification === true || parentDir.canonical !== evalDir.canonical;\n   185\t  const privatePaths = [tasksDir, ...(includeParent ? [path.dirname(tasksDir)] : []), ...extraPrivatePaths];\n   186\t  const protectedPaths = [];\n   187\t  for (const item of privatePaths) {\n   188\t    const resolved = resolveCanonicalOrLexical(item);\n   189\t    if (!resolved) return \"qualification private corpus paths must be absolute\";\n   190\t    protectedPaths.push({ source: item, resolved, exists: fs.existsSync(item) });\n   191\t  }\n   192\t  for (const item of [...runtimePaths, ...runtimeRoots]) {\n   193\t    if (typeof item !== \"string\" || !path.isAbsolute(item)) return \"qualification runtime paths and roots must be absolute\";\n   194\t    if (!fs.existsSync(item)) return `qualification runtime path is unavailable: ${item}`;\n   195\t  }\n   196\t  const mountSources = candidateSandboxHostMountSources({\n   197\t    readOnlyPaths: runtimePaths,\n   198\t    supervisorPath: mountContext.supervisorPath ?? path.join(ROOT, \"eval/agent_sandbox.mjs\"),\n   199\t    nodeRuntime: mountContext.nodeRuntime ?? process.execPath,\n   200\t    cwd: mountContext.cwd,\n   201\t    home: mountContext.home,\n   202\t    proxyDirectory: mountContext.proxyDirectory,\n   203\t  });\n   204\t  const hostSources = [\n   205\t    ...mountSources.systemDirectories,\n   206\t    ...mountSources.systemFiles,\n   207\t    ...mountSources.systemEtcDirectories,\n   208\t    ...mountSources.toolchainDirectories,\n   209\t    ...Object.values(mountSources.runtimeSources).flatMap((value) => Array.isArray(value) ? value : value === null ? [] : [value]),\n   210\t  ];\n   211\t  const candidatePaths = [...new Set([...hostSources, ...runtimePaths, ...runtimeRoots])];\n   212\t  const candidates = [];\n   213\t  for (const item of candidatePaths) {\n   214\t    const resolved = resolveCanonicalOrLexical(item);\n   215\t    if (!resolved) return \"qualification runtime paths and roots must be absolute\";\n   216\t    candidates.push({ source: item, resolved });\n   217\t  }\n   218\t  for (const candidate of candidates) {\n   219\t    for (const protectedPath of protectedPaths) {\n   220\t      if (overlapVariants(candidate.resolved, protectedPath.resolved)) {\n   221\t        return `qualification runtime mount overlaps protected corpus material: ${candidate.source}`;\n   222\t      }\n   223\t    }\n   224\t  }\n   225\t  const unavailablePrivate = protectedPaths.find((item) => !item.exists);\n     1\t// Black-box qualification oracle: expected values stay in the evaluator process and are never mounted into candidate\n     2\t// execution. The candidate receives one public input at a time and returns JSON for comparison by this parent.\n     3\timport fs from \"node:fs\";\n     4\timport path from \"node:path\";\n     5\timport { isDeepStrictEqual } from \"node:util\";\n     6\timport { confinedPath } from \"./isolate.mjs\";\n     7\timport { isolatedProcessArgv, oracleToolchainsProblem, runIsolatedProcess } from \"./isolate.mjs\";\n     8\t\n     9\tconst RUNTIMES = Object.freeze({ node: process.execPath, python3: \"/usr/bin/python3\" });\n    10\tconst MAX_ORACLE_BYTES = 1_048_576;\n    11\tconst MAX_CASES = 256;\n    12\tconst MAX_CASE_BYTES = 16_384;\n    13\tconst MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 32_768;\n    14\tconst MAX_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n    15\tconst MAX_TOTAL_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000;\n    16\t\n    17\t/** Validate a frozen black-box oracle definition without exposing its case contents in diagnostics. */\n    18\texport function blackBoxOracleProblem(check, taskDir, candidateFixture = null) {\n    19\t  if (!check || check.protocol !== \"blackbox-v1\") return \"hidden oracle protocol must be blackbox-v1\";\n    20\t  if (Object.keys(check).some((key) => ![\"protocol\", \"casesFile\", \"runtime\", \"argv\", \"timeoutMs\", \"totalTimeoutMs\", \"maxOutputBytes\"].includes(key))) {\n    21\t    return \"hidden black-box oracle has unsupported fields\";\n    22\t  }\n    23\t  if (typeof check.casesFile !== \"string\") return \"hidden black-box oracle casesFile is required\";\n    24\t  let casesPath;\n    25\t  try { casesPath = confinedPath(taskDir, check.casesFile); }\n    26\t  catch { return \"hidden black-box oracle casesFile is unsafe\"; }\n    27\t  if (candidateFixture) {\n    28\t    let fixturePath;\n    29\t    try { fixturePath = confinedPath(taskDir, candidateFixture); }\n    30\t    catch { return \"candidate fixture path is unsafe\"; }\n    31\t    const relative = path.relative(fixturePath, casesPath);\n    32\t    if (relative === \"\" || (!relative.startsWith(`..${path.sep}`) && relative !== \"..\" && !path.isAbsolute(relative))) {\n    33\t      return \"hidden black-box oracle casesFile must be outside the candidate fixture\";\n    34\t    }\n    35\t  }\n    36\t  try {\n    37\t    const stat = fs.statSync(casesPath);\n    38\t    if (!stat.isFile() || stat.size > MAX_ORACLE_BYTES) return \"hidden black-box oracle case file is not a bounded regular file\";\n    39\t    const rows = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(casesPath, \"utf8\"));\n    40\t    if (!Array.isArray(rows) || rows.length < 1 || rows.length > MAX_CASES) return \"hidden black-box oracle case count is outside the allowed range\";\n    41\t    for (const row of rows) {\n    42\t      if (!row || typeof row !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(row) || Object.keys(row).sort().join(\",\") !== \"expected,input\") {\n    43\t        return \"hidden black-box oracle cases must contain only input and expected fields\";\n    44\t      }\n    45\t      if (Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(row), \"utf8\") > MAX_CASE_BYTES) return \"hidden black-box oracle case exceeds the size limit\";\n    46\t    }\n    47\t  } catch {\n    48\t    return \"hidden black-box oracle cases are unavailable or malformed\";\n    49\t  }\n    50\t  if (!Object.hasOwn(RUNTIMES, check.runtime)) return \"hidden black-box oracle runtime is not allowlisted\";\n    51\t  if (check.runtime === \"python3\") {\n    52\t    const toolchainProblem = oracleToolchainsProblem();\n    53\t    if (toolchainProblem) return toolchainProblem;\n    54\t  }\n    55\t  if (!Array.isArray(check.argv) || check.argv.length !== 1 || typeof check.argv[0] !== \"string\" || check.argv[0].includes(\"\\0\") || Buffer.byteLength(check.argv[0]) > 1024) {\n    56\t    return \"hidden black-box oracle argv must name only one candidate entrypoint\";\n    57\t  }\n    58\t  const entrypoint = check.argv[0];\n    59\t  if (path.posix.isAbsolute(entrypoint) || entrypoint.startsWith(\"-\") || entrypoint.includes(\"\\\\\") || entrypoint.split(\"/\").some((part) => !part || part === \".\" || part === \"..\")) {\n    60\t    return \"hidden black-box oracle argv must be a normalized relative candidate entrypoint\";\n    61\t  }\n    62\t  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(check.timeoutMs) || check.timeoutMs < 100 || check.timeoutMs > MAX_TIMEOUT_MS) return \"hidden black-box oracle timeout is outside the allowed range\";\n    63\t  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(check.totalTimeoutMs) || check.totalTimeoutMs < check.timeoutMs || check.totalTimeoutMs > MAX_TOTAL_TIMEOUT_MS) return \"hidden black-box oracle total timeout is outside the allowed range\";\n    64\t  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(check.maxOutputBytes) || check.maxOutputBytes < 2 || check.maxOutputBytes > MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES) return \"hidden black-box oracle output limit is outside the allowed range\";\n    65\t  return null;\n    66\t}\n    67\t\n    68\t/** Run candidate code as a black box. Only bounded summaries leave this function; raw output is discarded. */\n    69\texport async function runBlackBoxOracle(task, workspace, check, { resources = null, protectedCorpusPaths = [] } = {}) {\n    70\t  const problem = blackBoxOracleProblem(check, task.dir, task.fixture ?? null);\n    71\t  if (problem) throw new Error(problem);\n    72\t  const casesPath = confinedPath(task.dir, check.casesFile);\n    73\t  const entrypoint = confinedPath(workspace, check.argv[0]);\n    74\t  if (!fs.statSync(entrypoint).isFile()) throw new Error(\"black-box candidate entrypoint is not a regular file\");\n    75\t  const cases = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(casesPath, \"utf8\"));\ndocs/v1.0.0/EVIDENCE.md:23:| Toolchain | node v24.21.0, npm 11.19.0, cargo/rustc 1.95.0 (2026-03/04), Ubuntu with kernel 6.8.0-142, glibc 2.39, Intel Xeon X5650 (12 threads), 46 GB RAM |\ndocs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md:200:| REQ-REL-111 | Audit; DEC-12 | CI matrix for Node 22/24; release, reproducibility and evaluator builds use Node 22; pinned toolchain and MSRV, engines field, dist freshness (untracked). | compatibility | `ci.yml`, `release.yml`, `repro.yml` | At exact candidate `7c46a6d52095588f48579d9a96c5b2d526bdb3d9`, PR #150 CI run `39866110578` passed both required matrix jobs (Node 22 job `120128589470`; Node 24 job `120128589221`). The same revision declares Node `>=22` in both package manifests, uses pinned Rust/MSRV, sets release and reproducibility packaging to Node 22, and runs the `cli/dist` freshness check in the matrix and package workflows. Exact-candidate Linux packaging remains a separate active source gate; this row covers ACC-SUP-05 only. | tested | ACC-SUP-05 | RR | REL-13, MNT-01 | SPEC-07 / PLAN-07 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:14:- new `about.toml`, `about.hbs`, `deny.toml`, `rust-toolchain.toml`\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:25:| 1 | Add `rust-toolchain.toml` pinned to the current stable (1.95.0 at audit time), set `rust-version` in Cargo.toml, and add `\"engines\": {\"node\": \">=22\"}` (DEC-12). | root, `core/Cargo.toml`, `cli/package.json` | — |\ndocs/v1.0.0/plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:34:| 10 | Cross-runner reproducibility: a workflow_dispatch job builds on ubuntu-22.04 and ubuntu-24.04 with the pinned toolchain and compares the outputs. Run it once for the RC. | new `.github/workflows/repro.yml` | 1 |\ndocs/v1.0.0/AUDIT.md:23:| Toolchain at audit | node v24.21.0, npm 11.19.0, cargo/rustc 1.95.0, Ubuntu (kernel 6.8), glibc 2.39 | RT |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:71:| REL-13 | S3 | CI coverage | .github/workflows/ci.yml:41-66; release.yml | SOURCE + RUNTIME (CI log). There is one OS/arch (ubuntu-22.04 x86_64) and Node 20 only on GitHub; Node 22/24 is not tested, while the developer runs 24.21.0. Rust uses `dtolnay/rust-toolchain@stable`, with no `rust-toolchain.toml` and no `rust-version` in Cargo.toml, so the toolchain floats. There is no `engines` field in package.json. Nothing asserts that tracked `cli/dist` equals a build of `cli/src`. There is no musl/arm/macOS, which matches the docs. Positive: the PTY e2e suites run in CI (905 tests, 0 skipped). | Breakage on newer Node or a future stable Rust is caught late. Reproducibility depends on whatever \"stable\" is on release day. | Add a Node matrix (20, 22, 24). Pin the toolchain file and MSRV. Add an `engines` field. Add a `git diff --exit-code cli/dist` step after the build. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:74:| MNT-03 | S4 | maintainability | Makefile:36-45 | SOURCE. `verify-reproducibility` writes fixed `/tmp/nexus-repro-{1,2}` and covers only cli/dist, not the release tarball. The real check lives in the workflows (`cmp` of two package builds, same machine and same toolchain). | Weak local repro check. Cross-machine reproducibility is not proven. | Point the target at `package-linux.sh` twice + `cmp`, using mktemp. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:137:- **Cross-machine reproducibility is unproven.** The CI repro check runs on the same runner with the same toolchain. To qualify: build the tag on two different machines or runner images with a pinned toolchain, then `cmp` the tarballs and the published SHA256SUMS.\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/E_distribution.md:158:| E-R11 | CI matrix: Node 20/22/24, a pinned Rust toolchain plus MSRV, and a `cli/dist` freshness check. | compat | RELEASE-REQUIRED | Workflow jobs green; `git diff --exit-code cli/dist` step. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/A2_verification.md:57:| VER-06 | S3 | `run_tests` tool, `/test`, TDD, `nexus eval` | tester.ts:21-78 vs 109-138; tools.ts:589; agent.ts:4067-4090 | RUNTIME (tester_probe). `detectTestCommand` diverges from `discoverVerifier`. A Makefile containing only `pytest:` gives `make test` (discover: null). A repo with no manifest gives `npm test` (discover: null). `run_tests` results are recorded as objective verifier attempts under that command, so a nonsense FAILED attempt can mark a requirement's verification failed. `run_tests` uses a fixed 120 s instead of the verifier deadline. | Wrong command runs and is recorded. Long suites time out under run_tests but pass under the oracle. | RELEASE-REQUIRED: one selector (discoverVerifier plus filter); run_tests returns \"no verifier\" when there is none; use `verifierTimeoutSecs`. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:21:| REL-13 | CI covers Node 20 only (the developer runs 24.21.0) and a floating stable Rust. There is no `rust-toolchain.toml`, no MSRV, no `engines` field, and no check that `cli/dist` is fresh. | SOURCE + CI log |\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:44:5. **REQ-REL-111.** The CI matrix covers Node 22 and 24 (DEC-12: Node.js 20 is end of life and unsupported), with a pinned Rust toolchain (`rust-toolchain.toml`) and a declared MSRV (`rust-version`). It includes a `cli/dist` freshness check, with dist untracked per DEC-10. The package declares `\"engines\": { \"node\": \">=22\" }`.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:47:8. **REQ-REL-114.** Cross-machine reproducibility is demonstrated once for the RC: two different runner images with the pinned toolchain produce identical tarballs. This is RELEASE-REQUIRED evidence, not a code change.\ndocs/v1.0.0/specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md:71:| ACC-SUP-08 | Cross-runner reproducibility: tarballs built on ubuntu-22.04 and ubuntu-24.04 runners with the pinned toolchain are byte-identical, or the differences are documented and accepted as a DEC-10 follow-up. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:9:**Open source changes:** PR [#319](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/319), head `668721d8135d3a3332bca27b820caa5a4a76c7af`, merged as `a0356c4d8f0f0b9b943d4033aff59795fcab83b4` after its independent `ACCEPT` review and required Node 22/24/Linux, JS/TS, and CodeQL checks passed. Its post-merge CI and CodeQL checks are running. PR [#318](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/318), head `2c532d701c5b84b0d3308b02897520337addc36b`, has independent `ACCEPT` source review, but both Node matrix jobs failed because `eval_hardened.test.ts` could not find `go` while testing hidden-source isolation (exit 127); Linux was skipped. This is a CI toolchain-provisioning gap, not evaluator qualification evidence. PR [#322](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/322), head `d53caea7c480c5e88dff43c6e5d4cb8a16868400`, adds CI-only pinned Go/Rust toolchain provisioning and has independent `ACCEPT` review; its Node 22/24, JS/TS, and CodeQL checks pass, with Linux still running. PR [#320](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/320), head `2124538d21b84454319c5485140b266f11510a5a`, has independent `ACCEPT` review and passing Node 22/24, JS/TS, and CodeQL checks; Linux remains in progress. PR [#321](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/321) refreshes this checkpoint and has been rebased onto the new main; the previous head's checks do not qualify the rebased candidate, which requires fresh exact-head checks and independent review. None of PRs #318, #320, #321 or #322 is merged.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:15:**Disposition:** `NEXUS_V1_CONVERGENCE_ACTIVE`; acceptance `IN_PROGRESS`. Publication authorization remains granted but gated on complete mandatory acceptance. PR #319 is integrated; main post-merge CI is running. PRs #320 and #322 are in exact-head Linux validation, PR #321 is rebased with fresh checks pending, and PR #318 needs the toolchain change integrated and its exact-base qualification rerun. Exact-RC construction and qualification remain gated on pre-RC source readiness and complete independent custody acceptance; terminal operator evidence is still required before independent RC acceptance.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:25:**Qualification and custody:** Public `v1.0.0-rc.10` remains the latest prerelease; no stable `v1.0.0` release exists. The claims identify OpenCode as the prior/current custodian workflow, but do not verify the responsible human identity; SageBridge's 04:15 status says it is coordinating only. The successor T2 store remains 20/40 tasks; the remaining Go/Rust tasks need the reviewed, pinned-toolchain change integrated. Reported T3 digest/schema/isolation checks are 8/8, which is not full T3 task-quality qualification. At 04:23 UTC the OpenCode custodian holds SUITE for two-round setup/reference/oracle revalidation of the 20 converted tasks with isolated HOME; a live validator child process was observed. This is custody revalidation, not held-out qualification; no result or custody acceptance is recorded. Public Issue [#1](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/issues/1) remains open and unassigned; the latest metadata-only correction was posted at 04:18 UTC. T2 custody acceptance and qualification, full T3/T4 qualification, current-candidate provider and standalone qualification, fault injection, successor-RC reproducibility and independent exact-RC acceptance remain incomplete. RC10 evidence does not qualify a successor candidate.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:631:  - `blackbox-v1` oracle separation (`2c70d39`, PR #10) runs private oracle checks outside the candidate workspace while mounting the candidate workspace read-only (`--ro-bind`), superseding the historical shared-workspace oracle exposure noted in the 2026-10-17 review below.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:633:  - Kernel-enforced resource contract (`91406bd`, PR #19, `eval/resources.mjs`, `eval/cgroup_supervisor.mjs`, `scripts/ensure-ci-user-cgroups.sh`) executes each task phase (setup, candidate sandbox, hidden/regression, oracle) inside a transient delegated `systemd --user` scope with verified cgroup v2 `CPUQuota`, `MemoryMax`, `MemorySwapMax=0`, `TasksMax`, `memory.oom.group=1`, dedicated size-capped tmpfs `workspaceRoot` storage enforcement, and `cgroup.kill` process-tree cleanup verified to `populated == 0`. These controls are source-implemented, independently reviewed, and CI-tested (`1,152/1,152` on `0cee0b7`, run `39104943950`), not live qualification results. Qualification-host provisioning (`nexus-eval` account, narrow sudoers rule, dedicated tmpfs mount, and frozen `config.resources` profile) remains a prerequisite before running held-out T2/T3/T4 workloads.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:668:- A separate harmless synthetic canary reproduction against the pre-PR-#10 `runIsolated()` boundary confirmed a material hidden-oracle integrity defect at `e1759ca`: candidate-like code executed by a checker could read and overwrite a hidden file copied into the same writable `/workspace`; its canary appeared in captured output and would be persisted in `hidden.log`. No host escape was demonstrated, and no held-out payload or model candidate was used in that reproduction. (Subsequently remediated at the source level by `blackbox-v1` oracle separation in PR #10 `2c70d39`, workspace history isolation in PR #18 `96aeef2`, and kernel resource isolation in PR #19 `91406bd`.)\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:680:- `rust-toolchain.toml` is tracked and pins Rust `1.95.0` with `rustfmt` and `clippy`.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:708:| `REQ-REL-114` | open (workflow enforced at `86bdbe6`; RC run pending) | `.github/workflows/repro.yml` and `scripts/repro-identity.mjs` (PR #20 `86bdbe6`) enforce fail-closed tag/commit/clean-tree/toolchain/artifact identity across Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 builders, covered by `repro_identity.test.ts`. Live execution on an actual RC tag remains pending RC construction. | Final RC tag and `.github/workflows/repro.yml` run | Release engineering | Dispatch `repro.yml` against the exact `v1.0.0-rc.1` tag/commit and archive both runner manifests and comparison evidence. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:760:- Reproducibility workflow [40195749685](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/40195749685) built successfully on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 from the exact tag, but its official `compare` job **failed**. The comparator's earlier archive and compatibility checks passed; it then found that `metadata.tools` records npm `10.9.9` in the published RC SBOM versus npm `10.9.4` in both rebuild SBOMs. Because it stopped at that mismatch, the later `install.sh` comparison was not reached. A local comparison of only the runner identity artifacts passed but did not establish `REQ-REL-114 / ACC-SUP-08`; the official result is authoritative. `REQ-REL-114` remains open until the toolchain is aligned, the release/rebuild evidence is regenerated as needed, and a new exact-RC comparison passes.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:772:| Cross-runner `REQ-REL-114` | **Failed** on RC5: run 40195749685 `compare` reports npm-version metadata mismatch (published 10.9.9; both rebuilds 10.9.4). Builder archives match, but the SBOM does not. | Align and pin release/rebuild toolchains; independently review and integrate the fix; create the next correctly versioned RC only after pre-RC gates; rerun exact-artifact cross-runner comparison and retain its generated evidence. |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":889895550}},"started_at_ms":1793279797547,"completed_at_ms":1793279798438}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T13:16:38.571Z","ordinal":502921,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a187ce-2d2b-74d1-966a-9572a88bcc3d","call_id":"call_cca379a333ce42338224b7287aeb6617","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 7193)\nTotal output lines: 256\n\n     1\t// Checks that make an evaluation run valid before it starts (SPEC-10, REQ-QUAL-101/102/106): every task is well formed,\n     2\t// a qualification run records which model actually answered (a fingerprint, not an alias), and hidden tests are out of\n     3\t// the agent's reach when the agent runs as its own user.\n     4\timport fs from \"node:fs\";\n     5\timport path from \"node:path\";\n     6\timport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n     7\timport { createHash } from \"node:crypto\";\n     8\timport { assertNoSymlinks, candidateSandboxHostMountSources, confinedPath, runtimeMountProblem, sandboxAvailable } from \"./isolate.mjs\";\n     9\timport { blackBoxOracleProblem } from \"./oracle.mjs\";\n    10\timport { t3TaskProblems } from \"./t3.mjs\";\n    11\timport { resourceProbeProblem } from \"./resources.mjs\";\n    12\timport { t4FixtureProblems } from \"./t4_fixtures.mjs\";\n    13\t\n    14\texport const TIERS = [\"T1\", \"T2\", \"T3\", \"T4\"];\n    15\texport const CLASSES = [\"bugfix\", \"feature\", \"refactor\", \"debug\"];\n    16\texport const DIFFICULTIES = [\"easy\", \"medium\", \"hard\"];\n    17\tconst ROOT = path.resolve(path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), \"..\");\n    18\t\n    19\t/** Problems with one task.json (empty when valid). `qualification` adds the fields held-out tasks must carry. */\n    20\texport function validateTask(task, dir, { qualification = false } = {}) {\n    21\t  const problems = [];\n    22\t  const need = (cond, msg) => { if (!cond) problems.push(msg); };\n    23\t  need(typeof task.id === \"string\" && task.id === path.basename(dir), `id must equal its directory name (${path.basename(dir)})`);\n    24\t  need(TIERS.includes(task.tier), `tier must be one of ${TIERS.join(\", \")}`);\n    25\t  need(typeof task.objective === \"string\" && task.objective.trim().length > 0, \"objective is required\");\n    26\t  problems.push(...t3TaskProblems(task));\n    27\t  need(Boolean(task.fixture) !== Boolean(task.repo), \"exactly one of fixture or repo is required\");\n    28\t  if (task.fixture) {\n    29\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.fixture); }\n    30\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    31\t  }\n    32\t  if (task.repo) {\n    33\t    need(typeof task.repo.url === \"string\" && task.repo.url.length > 0, \"repo.url is required\");\n    34\t    try {\n    35\t      const repoUrl = new URL(task.repo.url);\n    36\t      need(repoUrl.protocol === \"https:\" && !repoUrl.username && !repoUrl.password, \"repo.url must use HTTPS without embedded credentials\");\n    37\t    } catch { need(false, \"repo.url must be a valid HTTPS URL without embedded credentials\"); }\n    38\t    need(/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/.test(task.repo.commit ?? \"\"), \"repo.commit must be a full 40-character commit id (pinned)\");\n    39\t  }\n    40\t  const hiddenCommand = typeof task.hidden?.command === \"string\" && task.hidden.command.length > 0;\n    41\t  const hiddenOracle = task.hidden?.protocol === \"blackbox-v1\";\n    42\t  need(hiddenCommand || hiddenOracle, \"hidden.command or hidden blackbox-v1 oracle is required\");\n    43\t  if (hiddenOracle) {\n    44\t    const oracleProblem = blackBoxOracleProblem(task.hidden, dir, task.fixture ?? null);\n    45\t    need(!oracleProblem, oracleProblem ?? \"hidden black-box oracle is invalid\");\n    46\t  }\n    47\t  // Legacy validators execute candidate modules in the same runtime. File hiding and a fresh workspace cannot prevent\n    48\t  // candidate code from inspecting or mutating that runtime, so they are never valid independent qualification.\n    49\t  if (qualification) need(hiddenOracle && !hiddenCommand, \"qualification requires the isolated blackbox-v1 hidden oracle; in-process legacy validators are not accepted\");\n    50\t  if (task.hidden?.dir) {\n    51\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.hidden.dir); }\n    52\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    53\t  }\n    54\t  if (task.regression?.dir) {\n    55\t    try { confinedPath(dir, task.regression.dir); }\n    56\t    catch (error) { need(false, error.message); }\n    57\t  }\n    58\t  for (const key of [\"steps\", \"minutes\"]) {\n    59\t    if (task[key] !== undefined) need(Number.isInteger(task[key]) && task[key] > 0, `${key} must be a positive integer`);\n    60\t  }\n    61\t  if (task.faults !== undefined) {\n    62\t    const kinds = [\"refuse\", \"overloaded\", \"stream_cut\", \"truncate\", \"context_overflow\", \"malformed_tool_json\"];\n    63\t    need(Array.isArray(task.faults) && task.faults.every((f) => kinds.includes(f?.kind) && Number.isInteger(f?.atRequest) && f.atRequest >= 1), `faults must be [{ kind: ${kinds.join(\"|\")}, atRequest: n >= 1 }]`);\n    64\t  }\n    65\t  if (task.t4RunnerFault !== undefined) {\n    66\t    need(task.tier === \"T4\" && task.t4RunnerFault === \"sigterm_resume\", \"t4RunnerFault is supported only as sigterm_resume on a T4 task\");\n    67\t  }\n    68\t  problems.push(...t4FixtureProblems(task));\n    69\t  if (…4793 tokens truncated…d-toolchain change integrated. Reported T3 digest/schema/isolation checks are 8/8, which is not full T3 task-quality qualification. At 04:23 UTC the OpenCode custodian holds SUITE for two-round setup/reference/oracle revalidation of the 20 converted tasks with isolated HOME; a live validator child process was observed. This is custody revalidation, not held-out qualification; no result or custody acceptance is recorded. Public Issue [#1](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/issues/1) remains open and unassigned; the latest metadata-only correction was posted at 04:18 UTC. T2 custody acceptance and qualification, full T3/T4 qualification, current-candidate provider and standalone qualification, fault injection, successor-RC reproducibility and independent exact-RC acceptance remain incomplete. RC10 evidence does not qualify a successor candidate.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:631:  - `blackbox-v1` oracle separation (`2c70d39`, PR #10) runs private oracle checks outside the candidate workspace while mounting the candidate workspace read-only (`--ro-bind`), superseding the historical shared-workspace oracle exposure noted in the 2026-10-17 review below.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:633:  - Kernel-enforced resource contract (`91406bd`, PR #19, `eval/resources.mjs`, `eval/cgroup_supervisor.mjs`, `scripts/ensure-ci-user-cgroups.sh`) executes each task phase (setup, candidate sandbox, hidden/regression, oracle) inside a transient delegated `systemd --user` scope with verified cgroup v2 `CPUQuota`, `MemoryMax`, `MemorySwapMax=0`, `TasksMax`, `memory.oom.group=1`, dedicated size-capped tmpfs `workspaceRoot` storage enforcement, and `cgroup.kill` process-tree cleanup verified to `populated == 0`. These controls are source-implemented, independently reviewed, and CI-tested (`1,152/1,152` on `0cee0b7`, run `39104943950`), not live qualification results. Qualification-host provisioning (`nexus-eval` account, narrow sudoers rule, dedicated tmpfs mount, and frozen `config.resources` profile) remains a prerequisite before running held-out T2/T3/T4 workloads.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:668:- A separate harmless synthetic canary reproduction against the pre-PR-#10 `runIsolated()` boundary confirmed a material hidden-oracle integrity defect at `e1759ca`: candidate-like code executed by a checker could read and overwrite a hidden file copied into the same writable `/workspace`; its canary appeared in captured output and would be persisted in `hidden.log`. No host escape was demonstrated, and no held-out payload or model candidate was used in that reproduction. (Subsequently remediated at the source level by `blackbox-v1` oracle separation in PR #10 `2c70d39`, workspace history isolation in PR #18 `96aeef2`, and kernel resource isolation in PR #19 `91406bd`.)\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:680:- `rust-toolchain.toml` is tracked and pins Rust `1.95.0` with `rustfmt` and `clippy`.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:708:| `REQ-REL-114` | open (workflow enforced at `86bdbe6`; RC run pending) | `.github/workflows/repro.yml` and `scripts/repro-identity.mjs` (PR #20 `86bdbe6`) enforce fail-closed tag/commit/clean-tree/toolchain/artifact identity across Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 builders, covered by `repro_identity.test.ts`. Live execution on an actual RC tag remains pending RC construction. | Final RC tag and `.github/workflows/repro.yml` run | Release engineering | Dispatch `repro.yml` against the exact `v1.0.0-rc.1` tag/commit and archive both runner manifests and comparison evidence. |\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:760:- Reproducibility workflow [40195749685](https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/actions/runs/40195749685) built successfully on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 from the exact tag, but its official `compare` job **failed**. The comparator's earlier archive and compatibility checks passed; it then found that `metadata.tools` records npm `10.9.9` in the published RC SBOM versus npm `10.9.4` in both rebuild SBOMs. Because it stopped at that mismatch, the later `install.sh` comparison was not reached. A local comparison of only the runner identity artifacts passed but did not establish `REQ-REL-114 / ACC-SUP-08`; the official result is authoritative. `REQ-REL-114` remains open until the toolchain is aligned, the release/rebuild evidence is regenerated as needed, and a new exact-RC comparison passes.\ndocs/v1.0.0/READINESS.md:772:| Cross-runner `REQ-REL-114` | **Failed** on RC5: run 40195749685 `compare` reports npm-version metadata mismatch (published 10.9.9; both rebuilds 10.9.4). Builder archives match, but the SBOM does not. | Align and pin release/rebuild toolchains; independently review and integrate the fix; create the next correctly versioned RC only after pre-RC gates; rerun exact-artifact cross-runner comparison and retain its generated evidence. |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18771-cd08-7c60-9b2e-7bad123756d6","create_time":1793279798.571503}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
